## The problem

Posting on a schedule and following up on engagement are both jobs where the work itself
is trivial and the timing is the whole point. Done by hand, it means somebody is awake
at the right moment, every single time, and eventually they are not.

## Scheduling that survives its own failures

The service runs scheduled jobs behind FastAPI. "Post at 09:00" is the easy half of the
requirement. "Post exactly once, even if the worker restarted at 08:59" is the half that
shapes the design.

Scheduled actions are keyed and idempotent, so a retry after a crash does not publish
the same thing twice. Retries back off instead of hammering an API that is already
refusing. And failures are loud: a job that could not finish surfaces where someone will
see it, rather than settling into a log file. A late post is recoverable. A missed post
that nobody knew about is not.

## Working against the social platform APIs

The service connects directly to the social media APIs, which is where most of the real
difficulty lives. Every platform authenticates differently, expires its tokens on its own
schedule, publishes its own rate limits and then enforces stricter ones in practice, and
accepts a slightly different shape of post. Tokens have to be refreshed before a job
needs them rather than after a failure, because a token that expires at 3am takes the
morning's posts with it.

Each platform sits behind an adapter with one internal interface, so the scheduling logic
never learns platform-specific rules. Adding a platform means writing one adapter, and a
platform changing its API means editing one file.

## Stack

Python and FastAPI on AWS, using EC2, S3, RDS and Lambda, integrated with the social
media platform APIs.
